00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:01.690 align:start position:0%
 
foreign  the British economy is in crisis  the government has lost control of the  British economy in his mini budget  Britain's new Chancellor quasi-courteng  announced 45 billion pounds of unfunded  tax cuts causing the pound to plummet  the British pound has fallen to its  lowest level ever against the US dollar  the city of London woke up to a currency  crisis shocking the markets and leading  the bank of England to intervene the  bank of England has said that it will  buy as many long-dated government bonds  as required the international monetary  fund has slammed the government's plan  how bad is this and is there worse to  come as the markets rejected carsonomics  prime minister  our Britain economics editor Samaya  Keynes explains what's happening  the past week has been a Monumental week  for the British economy the government  announced regulatory reforms and also  tax cuts income tax cuts scrapping a  planned increase in corporation tax  changes to Bankers bonuses I think it's  the understatement of the century to say  that this went down extremely badly The  Fallout has been extraordinarily  embarrassing for a government that is  only a few weeks old there are really  big questions about the seriousness of  the government's handle on the public  finances and the extent to which it's in  control but the government doesn't  operate in a vacuum it looks like the  bank of England is going to respond to  this fiscal statement by raising  interest rates even more aggressively  than it was planning before that means  more expensive mortgages for households  that means house prices potentially  falling and it generally means depressed  demand and potentially a recession  fixing all of this restoring credibility  would require an astonishing U-turn it  would require reversing some of the tax  cuts the government has said that it  will publish its fiscal rules alongside  an independent forecast of the public  finances in late November that needs to  happen sooner now neither of those look  like they're going to happen what's more  likely is that the government is going  to ask departments to find efficiency  savings that means Cuts it's been  reported that they may be considering  real terms Cuts in benefits that will be  politically extraordinarily difficult  when they've just announced tax cuts for  the richest the political difficulty of  restoring credibility and and giving  this sense to International investors  that things are under control is just  really really difficult right now all of  this is happening in a broader economic  context of course borrowing costs to  governments arising around the world  Britain is not the only one by any means  but the increase in its borrowing costs  has been remarkably large there is a  Britain specific component here it's not  right to say that this is just as part  of a general Trend it is also true  though that if those problems are fixed  borrowing costs for the government would  still be rising in general the economic  environment is just becoming more  hostile it's becoming more hostile to  governments making mistakes and the  British government is feeling the pain  of that  thank you for watching to read more of  our coverage of the British economy  click the link and don't forget to  subscribe  [Music]

